# Production deployment

Four containers on one host: `app` (php-fpm), `nginx`, `queue` (`queue:work`) and
`scheduler` (`schedule:work`). The app, queue and scheduler run the same image and
differ only in the entrypoint compose gives them. The database and cache live in
their own containers, already running on a shared docker network.

```
Internet
  └─ host nginx (TLS, /etc/nginx/conf.d/<domain>.conf)   ← vps-nginx/
       └─ 127.0.0.1:3000
            └─ nginx container (:80)                     ← nginx/default.conf
                 └─ fastcgi app:9000
                      └─ app container (php-fpm)         ← entrypoint/app-entrypoint.sh
                            queue container              ← entrypoint/queue-entrypoint.sh
                            scheduler container          ← entrypoint/scheduler-entrypoint.sh
                                 ↳ external services: database, cache/queue, ...
```

Every file here ships with `myapp` and `example.com` placeholders. Replace them
before the first deploy.

## Files

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `Dockerfile` | Multi-stage build; `quality` stage runs Pint, `app` stage is last and ships as `:latest`. |
| `docker-compose.yml` | The four services, healthchecks, resource caps, log rotation, external network and volume. |
| `entrypoint/app-entrypoint.sh` | Dependency waits, migrations, cache warmup, `public/` publish, then `exec php-fpm`. |
| `entrypoint/queue-entrypoint.sh` | Cache priming, then `exec queue:work` as `www-data`. |
| `entrypoint/scheduler-entrypoint.sh` | Cache priming, then `exec schedule:work`. |
| `php/php-fpm.conf` | Pool sizing, slowlog, `ping.path`. Mounted as `zzz-custom.conf` so it beats `www.conf`. |
| `php/opcache.ini` | Production opcache, including `validate_timestamps=0`. |
| `php/php.ini` | Limits, timeouts, upload sizes. |
| `nginx/default.conf` | Container nginx: fastcgi to `app:9000`, dotfile denies. |
| `vps-nginx/site.conf.example` | Host nginx: TLS, proxy to `127.0.0.1:3000`, upstream keepalive, maintenance page. |
| `vps-nginx/maintenance.html` | Static page the host nginx serves while the app container is being replaced. |

No CI pipeline is included. Whatever builds the image has to produce the
environment file described below.

## First deploy

1. On the host, create the shared network and the storage volume:

   ```bash
   docker network create global_network        # skip if it already exists
   docker volume create myapp_storage         # or reuse an existing volume name
   ```

2. Replace `example.com` and `myapp` in `vps-nginx/site.conf.example`, install it
   as `/etc/nginx/conf.d/<domain>.conf`, and install the maintenance page:

   ```bash
   sudo install -D -m 644 vps-nginx/maintenance.html /var/www/maintenance/maintenance.html
   sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx
   ```

   TLS comes from certbot or an existing certificate.

3. Confirm `bootstrap/app.php` trusts the proxy. Without it every generated URL is
   `http://` and every client looks like the proxy:

   ```php
   ->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware) {
       $middleware->trustProxies(at: '*');
   })
   ```

   `at: '*'` is correct here, and only because the container port is published on
   `127.0.0.1`: nothing but the host nginx can reach it, so no untrusted client
   can forge the headers. Do not narrow it to the docker subnet — the container
   nginx rewrites `REMOTE_ADDR` to the real client with `real_ip_header`, so a
   subnet allow-list would match nothing and the headers would be dropped again.

4. Set `pm.max_children` in `php/php-fpm.conf` and the memory limits together, from
   real host numbers. See [Sizing](#sizing).

5. Audit the route cache locally. `route:cache` also runs in the entrypoint, so
   anything it rejects kills the new container after the old one has stopped:

   ```bash
   php artisan route:cache && php artisan route:clear
   ```

6. Write the deploy `.env` (see [Environment](#environment)), then:

   ```bash
   docker build -f docker/production/Dockerfile -t myapp:latest .
   docker compose -f docker/production/docker-compose.yml --env-file .env up -d
   ```

   Deploy with `up -d`, never `down`: compose then recreates only the services
   whose image changed, and the queue worker gets its `stop_grace_period` to
   finish the job in flight.

## Environment

`docker-compose.yml` reads its own variables from the file passed as
`--env-file`, and passes that same file to the containers as `env_file`, so one
file carries both groups.

Application keys, on top of the usual Laravel ones:

| Key | Why |
|---|---|
| `APP_ENV=production`, `APP_DEBUG=false` | `config:cache` bakes these in. |
| `APP_KEY` | Must be a real key, bound from a secret store, never committed. |
| `DB_HOST`, `DB_PORT` | Container name and port of the database. The entrypoint waits for them, and skips the wait when unset. |
| `REDIS_HOST`, `REDIS_PORT`, `REDIS_PASSWORD` | Same, plus an `AUTH`/`PING` probe. Leave `REDIS_HOST` unset if the app has no redis. |
| `LOG_CHANNEL=stderr` | Routes logs to `docker logs`, where compose rotates them. A file channel writes onto the storage volume instead. |
| `SESSION_DRIVER=database` | Survives container replacement, unlike `file`. Needs the `sessions` table. |
| `QUEUE_CONNECTION` | Whatever the queue worker should read. |

Compose keys, all optional with defaults in the compose file:

| Key | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `COMPOSE_PROJECT` | `myapp-production` | Compose project name. |
| `IMAGE_NAME` | `myapp` | Image the three roles run, `:latest` tag. |
| `CONTAINER_PREFIX` | `myapp` | Container name prefix. |
| `APP_ENV_FILE` | `../../.env` | Env file, relative to `docker/production/`. |
| `HOST_IP` | `127.0.0.1` | Publish address. See the warning below. |
| `HOST_PORT` | `3000` | Loopback port the host nginx proxies to. |
| `NETWORK_NAME` | `global_network` | Existing external network. |
| `STORAGE_VOLUME` | `myapp_storage` | Existing external volume for `storage/`. |
| `APP_MEM_LIMIT`, `APP_MEM_RESERVATION`, `APP_CPUS` | `1500m`, `768m`, `1.5` | app caps. |
| `QUEUE_MEM_LIMIT`, `QUEUE_MEM_RESERVATION`, `QUEUE_CPUS` | `768m`, `256m`, `1.0` | queue caps. |
| `SCHEDULER_MEM_LIMIT`, `SCHEDULER_MEM_RESERVATION`, `SCHEDULER_CPUS` | `256m`, `128m`, `0.5` | scheduler caps. |
| `NGINX_MEM_LIMIT`, `NGINX_MEM_RESERVATION`, `NGINX_CPUS` | `128m`, `64m`, `0.5` | nginx caps. |
| `QUEUE_NAMES`, `QUEUE_TRIES`, `QUEUE_BACKOFF`, `QUEUE_TIMEOUT`, `QUEUE_SLEEP`, `QUEUE_MAX_JOBS`, `QUEUE_MAX_TIME`, `QUEUE_WORKER_MEMORY` | `default`, `3`, `10`, `60`, `3`, `500`, `3600`, `192` | `queue:work` flags. |

Two rules that bite:

- **`config:cache` breaks `env()` outside `config/`.** Once the config cache
  exists the framework never reads `.env` again, so `env('FOO')` in a controller,
  job or view returns `null` with no error. It cannot reproduce locally, because
  local development does not cache config. Use `env()` only inside `config/`, and
  `config('services.foo.key')` everywhere else.
- **UFW does not filter published container ports.** Docker writes a DNAT rule
  into the nat table's `DOCKER` chain, traversed before UFW's filter rules, so a
  container published on `0.0.0.0` is reachable from the internet even with
  `ufw deny` on that port. Setting `HOST_IP=0.0.0.0` publishes this stack to the
  internet and no UFW rule can take it back.

## Sizing

`APP_MEM_LIMIT` and `pm.max_children` move together:

```
max_children x 48MB + 128MB  <=  APP_MEM_LIMIT
```

Raising `max_children` alone converts a slow request into an OOM kill. Derive the
stack budget by paying the other tenants first, then split it app 57%, queue 29%,
scheduler 10%, nginx 4%:

```
stack budget = host RAM - database - cache - (OS + docker + CI reserve)
```

| Host RAM | DB + cache | OS reserve | Stack budget | app | queue | scheduler | nginx | max_children |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2 GB | 512m + 256m | 400m | ~850m | 512m | 256m | 128m | 96m | 8 |
| 4 GB | 768m + 384m | 600m | ~2.4g | **1500m** | **768m** | **256m** | **128m** | **24** |
| 8 GB | 2g + 512m | 800m | ~4.6g | 2800m | 1200m | 384m | 256m | 44 |
| 16 GB | 4g + 1g | 1g | ~10g | 6g | 2500m | 512m | 256m | 90 |

The bold row is what ships here. External service sizes are examples; use the real
ones, and re-derive after anything else lands on the host.

Then check the pool against the database. Laravel opens one connection per request
in flight, and postgres allows 97 by default:

```
max_children + queue containers + 5  <=  max_connections - 3
```

If it does not fit, lower `max_children`. Exceeding it fails as
`SQLSTATE[08006] FATAL: sorry, too many clients already` on some requests only.

## Timeout ladder

Keep this order, so nginx returns the 504 before php-fpm kills the worker:

```
nginx (container) fastcgi_read_timeout      60s
php.ini           max_execution_time        60
host nginx        proxy_read_timeout        65s
php-fpm           request_terminate_timeout 75s
```

## Deploy window

`docker compose up -d` stops the old app container and starts the new one.
Requests fail from that stop until php-fpm listens again, which is after the whole
entrypoint: dependency waits, `optimize:clear`, `storage:link`, `config:cache`,
publishing `public/`, migrations, then the route, view and event caches. Expect
15-45s, dominated by migrations.

nginx is not recreated, so it answers `fastcgi_pass app:9000` with 502 for that
whole window. The host nginx maps 502, 503 and 504 onto the static maintenance
page, which is why that page belongs to the host layer and not to the app.

Genuine zero-downtime needs two app containers and an upstream swap. This layout
trades that for a bounded, honest window.

## Verification

```bash
# 1. containers
docker ps -a --filter name=myapp --format 'table {{.Names}}\t{{.Status}}'

# 2. clean boot — expect every [entrypoint] step, ending at "starting php-fpm"
docker logs --tail=40 myapp_app

# 3. HTTP, both layers
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{time_total}s\n' http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/health
curl -s -w '\n' https://example.com/api/health

# 4. caches baked, debug off, no stale drivers
docker exec myapp_app php artisan about --only=environment,cache,drivers

# 5. queue and scheduler
docker logs --tail=15 myapp_queue
docker logs --tail=15 myapp_scheduler

# 6. capacity against pm.max_children, and nothing at its memory ceiling
nproc; free -m
docker exec myapp_app sh -c 'ps -o rss= -C php-fpm | sort -n | tail -1'
docker stats --no-stream --format 'table {{.Name}}\t{{.MemUsage}}\t{{.MemPerc}}\t{{.CPUPerc}}'
docker inspect -f '{{.Name}} oom={{.State.OOMKilled}} restarts={{.RestartCount}}' \
    myapp_app myapp_queue myapp_scheduler myapp_nginx

# 7. nothing exposed past loopback except the host nginx (:22, :80, :443 only)
ss -ltnp | grep -vE '127\.0\.0\.1|\[::1\]'

# 8. proxy headers: absolute URLs must be https, and logs must show real client IPs
docker logs --tail=3 myapp_nginx
```

Read the results carefully:

- `docker logs myapp_app | grep 'reached pm.max_children'` means the pool starved.
  Raise `max_children` and `APP_MEM_LIMIT` together, then re-check the connection
  ceiling.
- `MemPerc` steady above roughly 85% on any service is the next OOM kill, not
  headroom.
- On redis, `maxmemory-policy` must be `noeviction` or `volatile-*`. Any
  `allkeys-*` policy silently evicts queued jobs, because Laravel's redis queue
  keys have no TTL.
- A healthy `/api/health` is not proof the app works, only that it responds.
  Exercise one real authenticated flow before calling a deploy verified.
